  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-106775",
      "knowledge_point_id": "c4e9867f-15ba-5073-a20f-542045fced08",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Eigenvalue that Corresponds to a Given Eigenvector",
      "problem": "Determine if $v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}6 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?",
      "worked_solution": "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $v$ is an eigenvector, then there must exist a real number $\\lambda $ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ Hence, we have\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]=\\lambda [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis leads to the following system of equations for $\\lambda :$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}16=2\\lambda  \\\\ -8=-4\\lambda \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis system has no solution. Therefore, $v$ is not an eigenvector of $A.$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices_complete": true,
